.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- the browser's GPU, by WebGPU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #γ /dev

.B /dev/gpu/clone
.BI /dev/gpu/ n /ctl
.BI /dev/gpu/ n /data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #γ
gives programs the GPU of the computer that runs the browser, as WebGPU has it,
for computing: buffers, compute shaders in WGSL, and their dispatch.
It knows nothing of what the shaders compute.
It is shaped as
.IR draw (3)
is.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
makes a session, a directory
.IR n ,
and reads as
.IR n .
The session lasts while any of its files is open.
Each session has a WebGPU device of its own, with the adapter's limits,
and its buffers, shader modules and pipelines are its own.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
gives the adapter, a line for each of
.BR vendor ,
.BR architecture ,
.B device
and
.BR description ,
then the limits that matter for computing, a line for each with its value, such as
.EX
	maxStorageBufferBindingSize 4294967292
.EE
Where there is no WebGPU, in Node or in a browser without it,
.B ctl
reads
.B none
and a write to
.B data
fails.
.PP
A write to
.B data
holds messages, one after another; a message may go on into the next write.
Each is a letter, then its numbers, each 4 bytes, little-endian, then its bytes.
An
.I id
names a buffer, module or pipeline of the session; the program chooses it, and it must be free.
.TP
.BI b " id size usage"
Allocate a buffer of
.I size
bytes, zero.
.I Usage
is WebGPU's
.B GPUBufferUsage
bits, such as 0x80,
.BR STORAGE ;
.B COPY_SRC
and
.B COPY_DST
are always added.
.TP
.BI w " id off n bytes\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Write
.I n
bytes into buffer
.I id
at
.IR off ,
both multiples of 4.
.TP
.BI s " id n source\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Compile a shader module from WGSL.
An error in it fails the write with its line, column and text.
.TP
.BI p " id module n entry\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Make a compute pipeline of the module's entry point, with the bind group layout the shader declares.
.TP
.BI d " pipeline n id\fR[\fIn\fR] " "x y z"
Dispatch
.I x
by
.I y
by
.I z
workgroups of the pipeline, with buffer
.IR id [ i ]
at binding
.I i
of group 0.
.TP
.BI r " id off n"
Copy
.I n
bytes of buffer
.I id
from
.IR off ;
the next reads of
.B data
give them.
.TP
.BI f " id"
Free a buffer, module or pipeline.
.PP
A write's messages go to the GPU in one submission, in order:
a write into a buffer after a dispatch in the same write waits for the dispatch.
The write returns when the GPU has done them and the bytes its
.B r
messages asked for are in hand; reads of
.B data
then give those bytes, in the order asked, and an empty read after the last.
An error names the message and fails the write; the messages after it are not done.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add 1 to each of 64 integers, as in C:
.IP
.EX
src = "@group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read_write> v: array<u32>;\en"
	"@compute @workgroup_size(64) fn main(@builtin(global_invocation_id) i: vec3u) "
	"{ v[i.x] += 1u; }";
.EE
then write
.BI b " 1 256 128"\fR,
.BI w " 1 0 256 " ...\fR,
.BI s " 2 " ...\fR,
.BI p " 3 2 4 " main\fR,
.BI d " 3 1 1 1 1 1"
and
.BI r " 1 0 256"
in one write, and read 256 bytes.
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B k/src/kernel/gpu.ts
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR draw (3),
.IR zero (1)
.br
.B https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/
.br
.B https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/
.SH BUGS
A session cannot share its buffers with another, nor wait on less than a whole write.
